    <code code="10154-3" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Chief complaint Narrative - Reported"/>
    <title>Chief Complaint</title>
    <text><paragraph>Three-month review of cyclic vomiting syndrome on amitriptyline. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
</component>

<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.4"/>
    <code code="10164-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Present illness Narrative"/>
    <title>History of Present Illness</title>
    <text><paragraph>Mr. Darwin returns eight weeks after starting amitriptyline. The dose was raised to 50 mg after two weeks and to 75 mg four weeks ago, by portal message. He had one episode two weeks into treatment; he took the sumatriptan spray and an ondansetron tablet within half an hour of the prodrome, and it settled in about ten hours without emergency care. He has had no episode in the four weeks since reaching 75 mg, and his cold three weeks ago passed without one. He has a dry mouth, mild constipation, and some grogginess in the first hour of the morning, and has gained 1 kg. He has no dizziness on standing, palpitations outside the prodrome, or urinary hesitancy. He practices the relaxation breathing most evenings and has not yet started cognitive behavioral therapy. No ECG was done at the 50 mg step, because the increase was made by portal message. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Element</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Medicine</td><td>Amitriptyline 75 mg at bedtime for four weeks</td></tr><tr><td>Episodes</td><td>One in eight weeks, aborted at the prodrome; none at 75 mg</td></tr><tr><td>Side effects</td><td>Dry mouth, mild constipation, morning grogginess; weight up 1 kg</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Relaxation breathing most evenings; trigger plan followed</td></tr></tbody></table></text>

  </section>
</component>

